--- body ---
## Context

`spec/DECISIONS.md` is the append-only ratification ledger. CLAUDE.md makes it
the authority: the author's rulings land there first and the other documents
are edited to match. Nothing enforces that an entry, once landed, is still
there.

**Confirmed by sabotage, not by reading.** Deleting the whole
"2026-08-29 — the plain-language rewrite is accepted" entry (881 characters, a
complete ratification) leaves `make check` fully green: ruff clean, 171 figure
pairs / 0 stale, governance 6 documents, terminology 45 entries, 55 tests
passed. No gate counts entries.

## Why this is not hypothetical

Two `spec/DECISIONS.md` merge conflicts landed in a single session
(2026-08-29): main's R7/C3 entry against the 0036 voice entry, then again
against the 0140 chunk-budget entry. Both had the same shape — two branches
appending to the same slot before `## Awaiting ratification` — and that shape
recurs by construction whenever two branches ratify in parallel, which is the
normal state of this repo.

The resolution is always "keep both". The failure mode is a conflict resolved
with "take theirs", or an editor that swallows a hunk: a ruling silently
vanishes and nothing downstream re-derives it, because the document is
append-only and there is no second copy. Both resolutions here asserted the
union in a script rather than trusting the merge, and that was a choice made
by hand.

## Scope, after the 2026-08-31 excess-weight ruling

One property, not two. **Monotonicity only: the ratified entry count never
decreases.**

Immutability — "a ratified entry is never edited again" — is NOT guarded.
Git holds every prior text of the ledger, and an edit to a ratified entry is
visible in the diff that makes it, which is where review already looks. Loss
is the case review cannot catch, because a conflict resolution that drops a
hunk looks exactly like a resolved conflict.

Also dropped: the per-entry digest census, the amendment-policy decision, and
registration in `check_governance.py`'s SCANNED set. A count is not a
document.

## Actions

1. Count ratified entries: lines matching `^\*\*\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2} — `
   inside the `## Ratified` section.
2. Commit the expected count in one line of the guard itself.
3. Fail when the live count is lower than the committed one, naming both
   numbers. A higher count is the normal case and refreshes the constant.

## Test

Red first, against the sabotage that found this: delete one ratified entry
from a fixture ledger and assert the guard fails. A guard that passes on the
deletion is the current state.

## Verification

- [ ] fails against a ledger with one entry deleted
- [ ] passes on the unmodified tree
- [ ] refreshing the count on a legitimate append is a one-line edit

## Invariants

- The ledger stays append-only and human-written; the guard counts, never
  rewrites.
- Figure-guard coverage of the `"decisions"` prose key is unchanged.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A guard fails on a lost entry, with the red case demonstrated first
- [ ] Wired into `make check`
